title: Ürgüplü Receives the Mandate
subtitle: April 29, 1972
new-page: true
max-visits: 1

= Ürgüplü Receives the Mandate

At around the same time as Ecevit's private meeting with President Sunay, developments took place at Çankaya Palace. With Erim having resigned from his position as Prime Minister, several political figures are hoping to be appointed in his place. Most notable among these is MGP Chairman Turhan Feyzioğlu, who believed he had a real chance at assuming the role of Prime Minister. However, it is not one of the party leaders that President Sunay chooses to take on the responsibility of forming a new government. Instead, he calls on Suat Hayri Ürgüplü, an independent senator, former Prime Minister and one of the most respected elder statesmen in Turkey, to take on the responsibility. Ürgüplü is called to Çankaya Palace and given the mandate to form a new government.

The son of Mustafa Hayri Efendi, who served as Sheikh ul-Islam in the closing years of the Ottoman Empire, Ürgüplü comes from a prominent Ottoman family with aristocratic roots. Throughout his long career, he has been involved in several political movements, including the CHP, the Democratic Party and the political circles of the AP. Having served as President of the Senate and as Prime Minister in 1965, Ürgüplü has gained a reputation as a dignified and independent statesman. In the wake of the collapse of İnönü's final coalition government, Ürgüplü was able to unite various right-wing parties in Parliament and govern the country until the general elections. This unique skill in uniting different political parties made Ürgüplü very appealing to both Sunay and the military. They hoped that Ürgüplü would be able to form a viable majority in Parliament once again, resolving the current government crisis. As such, for now at least, the task of solving the political crisis lies with someone who has previously had considerable success in doing exactly this.

